1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,408
Here is a line of code

2
00:00:01,408 --> 00:00:04,736
that a Harvard instructor committed,
live, in class.

3
00:00:05,056 --> 00:00:08,811
The distance from a point
to the origin is the square root

4
00:00:08,811 --> 00:00:10,624
of x squared plus y squared.

5
00:00:10,944 --> 00:00:13,419
This line takes
the square root of twice that.

6
00:00:13,739 --> 00:00:16,085
Nothing crashed; the app started fine.

7
00:00:16,405 --> 00:00:18,027
The answer was just wrong.

8
00:00:18,267 --> 00:00:19,632
You type git commit.

9
00:00:19,952 --> 00:00:22,735
Before that commit exists,
who gets to say no,

10
00:00:22,735 --> 00:00:24,731
and what can they actually catch?

11
00:00:25,051 --> 00:00:28,038
The lecture has a word
for whoever can say no:

12
00:00:28,038 --> 00:00:30,896
a gate, anything that can fail and stop you.

13
00:00:31,216 --> 00:00:34,992
Today we build one on your machine,
and watch it refuse a commit.

14
00:00:35,733 --> 00:00:38,692
Step back once:
in a machine learning system,

15
00:00:38,692 --> 00:00:42,901
the model is a few tiles in a big grid;
the rest is engineering.

16
00:00:43,221 --> 00:00:44,865
Today is that engineering:

17
00:00:44,865 --> 00:00:48,405
tests, formatters, linters,
and the hook that runs them.

18
00:00:48,650 --> 00:00:53,166
To follow along you need
your own clone of cheese-app-ci-cd,

19
00:00:53,166 --> 00:00:56,778
Docker Desktop running,
and Python 3.9 or newer.

20
00:00:57,098 --> 00:00:59,368
No cloud, no credentials, no bill:

21
00:00:59,368 --> 00:01:02,773
everything runs on your laptop,
which is the point.

22
00:01:03,017 --> 00:01:07,158
The tracker in the top left shows
which README step we are on;

23
00:01:07,158 --> 00:01:12,702
6 sections today: 4 from the cheese README,
1 for the guest lecture, 1 for cleanup.

24
00:01:13,022 --> 00:01:16,798
When the yellow your-turn tag appears,
pause and run the command.

25
00:01:17,118 --> 00:01:20,425
A gray extra tag marks
a check not in the README.

26
00:01:20,745 --> 00:01:23,753
Every command is in the study guide,
linked below.

27
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:26,549
The bug is real history.

28
00:01:26,869 --> 00:01:29,371
This repo's own log tells the story:

29
00:01:29,371 --> 00:01:33,333
messy code re-added on purpose,
then a crash, then a fix.

30
00:01:33,653 --> 00:01:36,508
Then the class demo slipped
the wrong square root in,

31
00:01:36,508 --> 00:01:37,963
and the revert took it out.

32
00:01:38,283 --> 00:01:44,597
An extra git log confirms the clone sits
at that last commit, from November 2025.

33
00:01:44,833 --> 00:01:46,860
Picture the point (3, 4).

34
00:01:47,180 --> 00:01:51,553
Its true distance from the origin is 5,
the radius of this circle.

35
00:01:51,873 --> 00:01:56,633
The buggy line returns
the square root of 50, about 7.07:

36
00:01:56,633 --> 00:01:58,721
it overshoots the circle.

37
00:01:59,041 --> 00:02:02,305
No crash, no error message,
only a wrong number.

38
00:02:02,550 --> 00:02:05,494
One existing test already
knew the right answer.

39
00:02:05,814 --> 00:02:11,638
It asks the app for the distance of (3, 4)
and insists on exactly 5.0.

40
00:02:11,958 --> 00:02:14,457
A test is an executable specification,

41
00:02:14,457 --> 00:02:18,337
and this one would have said
no before anyone saw the demo.

42
00:02:18,583 --> 00:02:22,167
When a test fails, there are two suspects,
the code and the test.

43
00:02:22,487 --> 00:02:26,092
The lecture's rule:
first believe the test, and fix the code.

44
00:02:26,412 --> 00:02:31,085
Rewriting the test to match a bug
turns your specification into a diary.

45
00:02:31,733 --> 00:02:33,077
What is worth testing?

46
00:02:33,397 --> 00:02:38,154
The lecture sorts inputs into three trays:
normal, edge, and invalid.

47
00:02:38,474 --> 00:02:42,290
For the power function here,
2 to the power 3 is normal;

48
00:02:42,290 --> 00:02:45,834
zero exponents,
infinity and not a number are edges.

49
00:02:46,154 --> 00:02:50,245
And for invalid input,
pytest.raises inverts the test:

50
00:02:50,245 --> 00:02:54,411
it passes only if power
on a string raises a TypeError.

51
00:02:55,450 --> 00:02:59,352
Now the lecture's vocabulary,
on one request:

52
00:02:59,352 --> 00:03:02,213
GET /euclidean_distance/?x=3&y=4.

53
00:03:02,533 --> 00:03:06,821
Inside the handler,
squaring x is one call, power(3, 2).

54
00:03:07,141 --> 00:03:11,407
A unit test skips the app entirely
and calls that one function alone.

55
00:03:11,727 --> 00:03:14,415
It is fast,
and when it fails you know where to look.

56
00:03:14,650 --> 00:03:17,658
An integration test wires
several of our parts.

57
00:03:17,978 --> 00:03:23,373
The test client hands the request straight
to the FastAPI app, in process:

58
00:03:23,373 --> 00:03:26,844
routing,
validation, handler and power all run,

59
00:03:26,844 --> 00:03:28,986
but no server and no network.

60
00:03:29,306 --> 00:03:33,018
Ten of those run here in under a second,
at the time of recording.

61
00:03:33,267 --> 00:03:36,830
A system test treats the running program
as a black box.

62
00:03:37,150 --> 00:03:39,276
The request leaves the test,

63
00:03:39,276 --> 00:03:44,211
crosses a real network wire to port 9000,
and comes back as JSON.

64
00:03:44,531 --> 00:03:48,179
It is the most honest test,
and the slowest and most fragile.

65
00:03:48,700 --> 00:03:51,149
Stacked, that is the testing pyramid:

66
00:03:51,149 --> 00:03:55,847
many cheap unit tests at the bottom,
few expensive system tests on top.

67
00:03:56,083 --> 00:03:58,856
The lecture adds a sharper lens: a border.

68
00:03:59,176 --> 00:04:02,590
A test is hermetic
when only your code can make it fail.

69
00:04:02,910 --> 00:04:06,408
Unit and integration tests live inside
that border.

70
00:04:06,728 --> 00:04:08,436
The system test crosses it:

71
00:04:08,436 --> 00:04:12,233
a live server can be down for reasons
that are not your bug.

72
00:04:12,467 --> 00:04:17,637
Two more kinds get faint rows: a smoke test,
which only asks whether it answers,

73
00:04:17,637 --> 00:04:21,256
and acceptance tests,
which this project lacks, for now.

74
00:04:21,576 --> 00:04:23,061
One note about kinds:

75
00:04:23,061 --> 00:04:26,952
a test's kind is named by what runs,
not by its folder.

76
00:04:27,272 --> 00:04:31,816
This repo's system test file
calls itself integration tests,

77
00:04:31,816 --> 00:04:36,815
and its pytest config defines markers,
labels for selecting tests,

78
00:04:36,815 --> 00:04:40,072
that nobody uses; markers return in part 2.

79
00:04:40,317 --> 00:04:43,240
Last picture: an empty table, four columns.

80
00:04:43,560 --> 00:04:47,762
What is checked, how, with which command,
and does it run on every commit.

81
00:04:48,082 --> 00:04:49,490
Part 1 fills it in.

82
00:04:50,533 --> 00:04:51,642
Time to type.

83
00:04:51,962 --> 00:04:55,643
The README says clone
or download from your repository,

84
00:04:55,643 --> 00:04:58,320
without an address, so here is the repo.

85
00:04:58,640 --> 00:05:01,754
Clone it, then cd into cheese-app-ci-cd.

86
00:05:02,074 --> 00:05:03,376
Pause and run both.

87
00:05:03,616 --> 00:05:07,456
An extra look around,
not in the README: ls -la.

88
00:05:07,776 --> 00:05:11,678
Everything today is here:
three test folders under tests,

89
00:05:11,678 --> 00:05:14,210
a pytest config, a pre-commit config,

90
00:05:14,210 --> 00:05:17,632
and the Dockerfile
that bakes them into one image.

91
00:05:17,866 --> 00:05:22,047
Now the README's first command:
sh docker-shell.sh.

92
00:05:22,367 --> 00:05:25,901
It builds the image,
about a minute at the time of recording,

93
00:05:25,901 --> 00:05:28,682
and drops you into a shell
inside the container.

94
00:05:29,002 --> 00:05:30,154
Pause and run it.

95
00:05:30,474 --> 00:05:35,488
Read the prompt: the user is app,
not root, and the ID changes every run.

96
00:05:36,933 --> 00:05:40,058
Inside, start the server:

97
00:05:40,058 --> 00:05:47,557
uvicorn api.service:app
--host 0.0.0.0 --port 9000 --reload.

98
00:05:47,877 --> 00:05:50,741
When it says Application startup complete,

99
00:05:50,741 --> 00:05:54,832
open the three README URLs in your browser;
pause and do it.

100
00:05:55,152 --> 00:05:58,069
Here three curl calls stand
in for the browser:

101
00:05:58,069 --> 00:06:00,613
welcome, health, and the distance answer.

102
00:06:01,650 --> 00:06:06,194
One small gap:
the README says this endpoint returns 5.0.

103
00:06:06,514 --> 00:06:09,015
Really it returns a JSON object

104
00:06:09,015 --> 00:06:12,807
whose distance field is 5.0,
as the code shows.

105
00:06:14,250 --> 00:06:17,514
Stop the server with Ctrl-C, then type exit.

106
00:06:17,834 --> 00:06:23,085
Because the shell script used --rm,
nothing is left behind;

107
00:06:23,085 --> 00:06:26,111
an extra docker ps -a confirms it.

108
00:06:26,431 --> 00:06:28,339
One warning that pays off soon:

109
00:06:28,339 --> 00:06:31,231
your files are not mounted
into this container.

110
00:06:31,551 --> 00:06:35,218
The --reload flag watches the copy baked
into the image,

111
00:06:35,218 --> 00:06:39,146
so an edit on your laptop changes nothing
until you rebuild.

112
00:06:40,183 --> 00:06:44,322
Part 2 hunts for quality issues
with two tools not to confuse.

113
00:06:44,642 --> 00:06:49,207
A formatter rewrites code into one layout
and never changes behavior.

114
00:06:49,527 --> 00:06:51,090
A linter only reports:

115
00:06:51,090 --> 00:06:54,711
style issues,
likely bugs, things needing a person.

116
00:06:55,031 --> 00:06:56,759
Format first, then lint.

117
00:06:57,616 --> 00:07:01,285
First build the image, cheese-app-api:local.

118
00:07:01,605 --> 00:07:02,979
Every step says CACHED:

119
00:07:02,979 --> 00:07:05,787
the shell script already
built this Dockerfile.

120
00:07:06,107 --> 00:07:07,259
Pause and run it.

121
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:14,476
Now the linter, inside the image:
flake8 --max-line-length=120 api/.

122
00:07:14,796 --> 00:07:15,948
Pause and run it.

123
00:07:16,268 --> 00:07:20,351
Two findings in utils.py,
a missing space after a comma,

124
00:07:20,351 --> 00:07:23,777
and too many blank lines:
mess left on purpose.

125
00:07:24,016 --> 00:07:26,064
Then black, in --check --diff mode.

126
00:07:26,384 --> 00:07:27,536
Pause and run it.

127
00:07:27,856 --> 00:07:31,056
It reports 2 files would be reformatted,
not one.

128
00:07:31,376 --> 00:07:35,387
This command gives black no width,
so it falls back

129
00:07:35,387 --> 00:07:40,891
to its default of 88 and also folds
a 95 character line in service.py.

130
00:07:41,133 --> 00:07:42,221
Watch the trap.

131
00:07:42,541 --> 00:07:46,480
At width 88,
black folds that line into three;

132
00:07:46,480 --> 00:07:48,706
at 120, it unfolds to one.

133
00:07:49,026 --> 00:07:54,783
The hook and CI here both use 120,
but several README commands pass no width,

134
00:07:54,783 --> 00:07:57,773
so the same file gets different answers.

135
00:07:58,093 --> 00:08:01,720
Always pass 120, or set it once in config.

136
00:08:06,966 --> 00:08:11,119
Option B runs the same checks
on your laptop:

137
00:08:11,119 --> 00:08:15,457
install black and flake8 with pip,
on the host,

138
00:08:15,457 --> 00:08:19,425
26.10.1 and 7.4.1 at the time of recording.

139
00:08:19,745 --> 00:08:20,897
Pause and run it.

140
00:08:21,217 --> 00:08:24,990
If pip refuses
with externally-managed-environment,

141
00:08:24,990 --> 00:08:26,716
as Homebrew Pythons do,

142
00:08:26,716 --> 00:08:30,243
use any lecture 1 virtual environment,
or pipx;

143
00:08:30,243 --> 00:08:32,758
the study guide has the one-liner.

144
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:34,301
Pause and run both.

145
00:08:34,621 --> 00:08:39,557
The local flake8, at its default of 79,
adds a third finding,

146
00:08:39,557 --> 00:08:41,853
that same 95 character line;

147
00:08:41,853 --> 00:08:45,544
local black wants
the same two files changed.

148
00:08:46,816 --> 00:08:48,352
What can a formatter fix?

149
00:08:48,672 --> 00:08:52,066
On the messy service.py
from the repo's history,

150
00:08:52,066 --> 00:08:56,450
black sweeps away almost every finding,
and exactly 2 survive:

151
00:08:56,450 --> 00:08:59,985
an unused import,
which a formatter cannot delete,

152
00:08:59,985 --> 00:09:03,520
and a long quoted sentence
black must not rewrite.

153
00:09:03,840 --> 00:09:05,739
The class fixed both by hand.

154
00:09:07,783 --> 00:09:11,367
Before running tests in bulk,
meet the machine that finds them.

155
00:09:11,687 --> 00:09:15,748
The config file pytest.ini sets the rules:

156
00:09:15,748 --> 00:09:22,226
files named test_*.py, classes named Test*,
functions named test_*.

157
00:09:22,546 --> 00:09:27,278
It also adds options to every run,
and the one to remember is -ra,

158
00:09:27,278 --> 00:09:30,503
the summary of everything
not plainly passed.

159
00:09:30,750 --> 00:09:33,645
Watch the collector sweep the tests folder:

160
00:09:33,645 --> 00:09:36,808
every matching name lights up,
26 tests in all.

161
00:09:37,466 --> 00:09:38,810
Something to ponder.

162
00:09:39,130 --> 00:09:42,554
A later README part appends
the line def broken():

163
00:09:42,554 --> 00:09:46,319
assert False to a test file,
hoping to break the build.

164
00:09:46,639 --> 00:09:48,538
Will the unit test run go red?

165
00:09:57,383 --> 00:10:01,500
No. Watch the collector:
the appended name stays dark.

166
00:10:01,820 --> 00:10:06,812
The name does not match test_*,
so it is never collected, never run.

167
00:10:07,132 --> 00:10:10,524
To really break the build,
call it test_broken.

168
00:10:10,844 --> 00:10:13,490
Discovery by convention cuts both ways.

169
00:10:13,733 --> 00:10:16,250
Three more pytest verbs in 30 seconds.

170
00:10:16,570 --> 00:10:20,261
A plain assert is rewritten
so a failure shows both values.

171
00:10:20,581 --> 00:10:24,208
For floats,
pytest.approx compares with a tolerance.

172
00:10:24,528 --> 00:10:29,220
And pytest.raises passes
only when the block raises the named error:

173
00:10:29,220 --> 00:10:31,290
the invalid tray from earlier.

174
00:10:31,533 --> 00:10:35,202
One gift from the lecture
that this repo does not use yet.

175
00:10:35,522 --> 00:10:40,066
The integration tests build
their test client once, as a module global.

176
00:10:40,386 --> 00:10:42,008
A fixture would do it better.

177
00:10:42,328 --> 00:10:46,509
A test asks for client by name,
and pytest builds and injects it.

178
00:10:46,829 --> 00:10:49,721
A setting says
whether each test gets a fresh one

179
00:10:49,721 --> 00:10:51,373
or the whole run shares one.

180
00:10:51,693 --> 00:10:56,173
And parametrize folds four copies
into one function with four inputs.

181
00:10:58,417 --> 00:11:02,148
Part 3 runs tests where the app lives,
inside the image,

182
00:11:02,148 --> 00:11:04,680
by three routes: docker run per suite,

183
00:11:04,680 --> 00:11:08,678
a live server for the system tests,
or an interactive shell.

184
00:11:08,998 --> 00:11:11,804
The Dockerfile copies pyproject.toml

185
00:11:11,804 --> 00:11:15,313
and installs the dependencies,
one fat layer.

186
00:11:15,633 --> 00:11:19,031
Then four thin copy lines bake
in the api code,

187
00:11:19,031 --> 00:11:22,502
the tests,
the pytest config and the entrypoint.

188
00:11:22,822 --> 00:11:25,069
The entrypoint puts pytest on PATH,

189
00:11:25,069 --> 00:11:29,372
and its exec line hands the container
to whatever command you pass.

190
00:11:29,692 --> 00:11:34,065
The container's exit code
is how docker run can fail a CI step.

191
00:11:34,300 --> 00:11:38,579
Build again, all cached,
then run the unit tests:

192
00:11:38,579 --> 00:11:43,644
docker run --rm, the image,
pytest tests/unit/: 11 passed.

193
00:11:43,964 --> 00:11:45,266
Pause and run both.

194
00:11:45,500 --> 00:11:49,055
The integration suite says 10 passed,
1 warning;

195
00:11:49,055 --> 00:11:52,241
both suites together, 21 passed, 1 warning.

196
00:11:52,561 --> 00:11:53,820
Pause and run them.

197
00:11:54,140 --> 00:11:59,699
The 1 warning is pytest 9's summary line
counting a warning this config hides,

198
00:11:59,699 --> 00:12:03,335
at the time of recording:
same tests, same results.

199
00:12:03,584 --> 00:12:07,872
Now the coverage run:
unit tests, measuring the api package.

200
00:12:08,192 --> 00:12:09,344
Pause and run it.

201
00:12:09,664 --> 00:12:14,315
Look at service.py: 0 percent;
the unit tests never import it.

202
00:12:14,635 --> 00:12:18,965
Coverage is a list of lines nobody
has asked about, not a score.

203
00:12:19,285 --> 00:12:20,458
Hold that thought.

204
00:12:20,700 --> 00:12:23,027
The gate table gets its first row:

205
00:12:23,027 --> 00:12:26,929
unit and integration tests,
hermetic, one docker command.

206
00:12:27,249 --> 00:12:29,831
Option 2 waits; it gets the next scene.

207
00:12:30,151 --> 00:12:32,552
Option 3 is the same interactively:

208
00:12:32,552 --> 00:12:36,807
docker-shell again,
the same pytest commands, the same counts.

209
00:12:37,127 --> 00:12:41,418
One trap:
the HTML report lands inside a container

210
00:12:41,418 --> 00:12:44,679
that --rm deletes, gone when you exit.

211
00:12:44,999 --> 00:12:46,770
Prefer the terminal report.

212
00:12:47,017 --> 00:12:52,105
This image ships its dev tools
as runtime dependencies, open ended pins,

213
00:12:52,105 --> 00:12:57,193
no lockfile; the guest lecture keeps them
in groups behind one lockfile.

214
00:12:57,513 --> 00:13:01,877
And the part 1 warning pays off:
edit utils.py on your laptop,

215
00:13:01,877 --> 00:13:05,257
and this container
still tests yesterday's copy.

216
00:13:05,577 --> 00:13:07,476
Rebuild, or you test the past.

217
00:13:10,017 --> 00:13:13,752
Option 2 is the system test,
and the lecture's question:

218
00:13:13,752 --> 00:13:16,353
can anything but our code make it fail?

219
00:13:16,673 --> 00:13:21,162
Start a live server,
detached, named api-server,

220
00:13:21,162 --> 00:13:23,500
on port 9000, with DEV=1.

221
00:13:23,820 --> 00:13:26,294
All you get back is a container ID.

222
00:13:26,614 --> 00:13:27,766
Pause and run it.

223
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,325
The README runs the tests immediately.

224
00:13:30,645 --> 00:13:32,104
We add one extra first:

225
00:13:32,104 --> 00:13:35,403
a loop curling the health endpoint
until it answers.

226
00:13:35,723 --> 00:13:39,179
CI waits too, in its own way;
you will see why soon.

227
00:13:39,499 --> 00:13:42,678
A second extra,
docker ps, shows the server up.

228
00:13:42,917 --> 00:13:45,229
Now the README's system command:

229
00:13:45,229 --> 00:13:48,698
docker run --network host,
pytest tests/system/.

230
00:13:49,018 --> 00:13:52,581
5 passed, and this time
the request really crossed the wire.

231
00:13:52,901 --> 00:13:54,053
Pause and run it.

232
00:13:55,834 --> 00:13:59,887
Something to ponder: the same command again,
with no server running.

233
00:14:00,207 --> 00:14:02,276
What exactly does pytest print?

234
00:14:11,117 --> 00:14:15,085
The README's next step answers it:
stop and remove the server.

235
00:14:15,405 --> 00:14:18,797
Pause and do it;
the cleanup doubles as our experiment.

236
00:14:19,034 --> 00:14:21,487
Here is the answer, as an extra run.

237
00:14:21,807 --> 00:14:25,285
1 passed, 4 skipped,
and the headline is still green.

238
00:14:25,605 --> 00:14:27,610
Four gray dots, not failures.

239
00:14:27,930 --> 00:14:35,567
Only the summary from -ra tells the truth:
SKIPPED, API not running at localhost:9000.

240
00:14:35,817 --> 00:14:37,012
Why does it skip?

241
00:14:37,332 --> 00:14:41,790
The test file probes the server once,
when pytest collects the module.

242
00:14:42,110 --> 00:14:44,495
That one answer marks the whole class:

243
00:14:44,495 --> 00:14:47,572
a server that comes up a second late
is too late.

244
00:14:47,892 --> 00:14:51,498
A smoke detector with its battery
removed will never beep,

245
00:14:51,498 --> 00:14:53,737
and that is not the same as no fire.

246
00:14:53,984 --> 00:14:58,853
On the border picture, 22 tests live inside:
the 21 from last scene,

247
00:14:58,853 --> 00:15:03,221
plus the one system folder test
that never needed the server.

248
00:15:03,541 --> 00:15:05,163
These 4 stand outside.

249
00:15:05,483 --> 00:15:10,453
The honest remedy is a marker the run
must select on purpose; part 2 shows it.

250
00:15:10,773 --> 00:15:14,136
CI's version is loud:
it curls the server first,

251
00:15:14,136 --> 00:15:17,429
and if the server is down,
the whole job fails.

252
00:15:17,749 --> 00:15:21,741
The system row enters the gate table
outside the turnstile:

253
00:15:21,741 --> 00:15:24,042
not on every commit, not hermetic.

254
00:15:25,083 --> 00:15:26,811
Part 4 installs the gate.

255
00:15:27,131 --> 00:15:30,906
Outside the container,
pip install pre-commit,

256
00:15:30,906 --> 00:15:33,450
4.7.0 at the time of recording;

257
00:15:33,450 --> 00:15:37,145
then, from the repo root,
pre-commit install:

258
00:15:37,145 --> 00:15:39,526
a hook file now sits in .git.

259
00:15:39,846 --> 00:15:41,147
Pause and run both.

260
00:15:41,383 --> 00:15:44,114
Git runs that hook file before every commit.

261
00:15:44,434 --> 00:15:49,703
It lives in .git, outside the repository:
every clone installs it again.

262
00:15:50,023 --> 00:15:53,650
It hands over to pre-commit,
which reads the repo's config.

263
00:15:53,900 --> 00:15:55,780
The config names nine hooks:

264
00:15:55,780 --> 00:15:59,404
seven hygiene guards,
from whitespace to private keys.

265
00:15:59,724 --> 00:16:03,110
Then black and flake8, both at width 120,

266
00:16:03,110 --> 00:16:08,726
both seeing only Python files under src:
the tests are never linted.

267
00:16:08,966 --> 00:16:11,441
Now the README's commit, on a clean tree.

268
00:16:11,761 --> 00:16:12,913
Pause and run it.

269
00:16:13,233 --> 00:16:16,572
The first run downloads
each hook's pinned environment,

270
00:16:16,572 --> 00:16:19,547
plus a harmless warning
at the time of recording.

271
00:16:19,867 --> 00:16:23,461
Then every hook says skipped and git
says nothing to commit:

272
00:16:23,461 --> 00:16:26,396
hooks check staged files,
and nothing was staged.

273
00:16:26,633 --> 00:16:31,412
To exercise them anyway,
pre-commit run --all-files.

274
00:16:31,732 --> 00:16:32,884
Pause and run it.

275
00:16:33,204 --> 00:16:37,749
Six hooks pass, one has no files to check,
then black fails:

276
00:16:37,749 --> 00:16:42,142
files were modified by this hook;
it reformatted utils.py.

277
00:16:42,462 --> 00:16:44,873
A quick ponder: flake8 runs next.

278
00:16:45,193 --> 00:16:47,454
Does it report part 2's two findings?

279
00:16:53,700 --> 00:16:58,991
No. Black had already rewritten the file,
so flake8 saw clean code and passed.

280
00:16:59,311 --> 00:17:01,444
Failed here does not mean broken;

281
00:17:01,444 --> 00:17:05,774
it means the hook changed files:
look at the change, then re-stage.

282
00:17:06,016 --> 00:17:07,988
Two extras close the loop:

283
00:17:07,988 --> 00:17:11,627
git diff shows the fix;
git add -u re-stages it.

284
00:17:11,947 --> 00:17:15,638
Commit again: seven hooks pass,
two have nothing to check,

285
00:17:15,638 --> 00:17:19,328
and a real commit enters history;
pause and walk the loop.

286
00:17:19,648 --> 00:17:22,766
The README's last variant,
plain pre-commit run,

287
00:17:22,766 --> 00:17:25,558
checks only staged files; everything skips.

288
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:31,103
A last demo, as an extra:
sed adds and stages an unused import;

289
00:17:31,103 --> 00:17:34,747
on a Mac,
sed -i needs empty quotes after it.

290
00:17:35,067 --> 00:17:36,903
The commit fails twice:

291
00:17:36,903 --> 00:17:40,894
black reformats the file,
and flake8 reports F401,

292
00:17:40,894 --> 00:17:46,011
which no formatter can fix;
git status confirms no commit exists.

293
00:17:46,331 --> 00:17:48,933
Restore the file: your machine now says no.

294
00:17:49,253 --> 00:17:51,685
But this gate never ran a single test.

295
00:17:52,005 --> 00:17:55,504
Part 2 builds one that does,
and shows what even it can miss.

296
00:17:56,550 --> 00:17:59,473
Part 2 is the guest lecture, by Rahul Dave.

297
00:17:59,793 --> 00:18:04,039
It builds the same gate
around a small machine learning project,

298
00:18:04,039 --> 00:18:07,820
babykev, which answers questions
about cheese and reports

299
00:18:07,820 --> 00:18:11,270
how confident it is;
a pure guess should score zero.

300
00:18:11,590 --> 00:18:13,510
No Docker until the very end.

301
00:18:13,830 --> 00:18:16,400
Two tools first, as extras:

302
00:18:16,400 --> 00:18:23,160
the package manager uv from lecture
2, 0.13.2 at the time of recording,

303
00:18:23,160 --> 00:18:26,587
and the command runner just, 1.59.0.

304
00:18:26,833 --> 00:18:31,569
Clone babykev and list its tags:
9 steps, one per lecture stage.

305
00:18:31,889 --> 00:18:34,208
The repo is its own slide deck,

306
00:18:34,208 --> 00:18:37,649
and git switch --detach
moves you to any step.

307
00:18:37,969 --> 00:18:39,271
Pause and run both.

308
00:18:39,517 --> 00:18:43,950
At step-00,
one command builds the environment:

309
00:18:43,950 --> 00:18:45,554
uv sync --locked.

310
00:18:45,874 --> 00:18:52,296
It installs Python 3.13.16 and the project
exactly as the lockfile says.

311
00:18:52,616 --> 00:18:55,432
Locked means stop rather
than silently upgrade.

312
00:18:55,752 --> 00:18:58,653
Its warning is harmless,
at the time of recording.

313
00:18:58,973 --> 00:19:02,856
The cheese repo froze an image;
babykev pins a lockfile.

314
00:19:03,176 --> 00:19:05,903
Typing just, alone, lists the recipes:

315
00:19:05,903 --> 00:19:09,490
one name per task,
for people, hook, and later CI.

316
00:19:09,810 --> 00:19:11,111
Pause and run these.

317
00:19:11,350 --> 00:19:13,974
Step 1 brings the contract and its tests.

318
00:19:14,294 --> 00:19:19,243
Run uv run pytest: 2 failed, 50 passed,
and the tests are right.

319
00:19:19,563 --> 00:19:24,128
One failure is a pure guess
scored as half confident;

320
00:19:24,128 --> 00:19:28,865
the other is 77 rounded probabilities
that sum to 0.77.

321
00:19:29,185 --> 00:19:32,875
Same rule as the cheese demo:
fix the code, not the tests.

322
00:19:33,195 --> 00:19:34,347
Pause and run it.

323
00:19:34,583 --> 00:19:38,338
Step 2 is format and lint at scale,
ruff in both roles.

324
00:19:38,658 --> 00:19:41,666
After formatting,
the linter counts 70 findings.

325
00:19:41,986 --> 00:19:44,290
Safe automatic fixes remove one.

326
00:19:44,610 --> 00:19:48,750
Unsafe fixes,
applied deliberately, remove 32 more;

327
00:19:48,750 --> 00:19:51,266
37 remain; those need a person.

328
00:19:51,586 --> 00:19:56,713
And the width war from part 1 is settled
in one line, line-length = 100,

329
00:19:56,713 --> 00:20:02,125
written once in pyproject.toml;
the formatter and the linter both follow it.

330
00:20:03,967 --> 00:20:07,602
Step 3 installs the gate with one recipe,
just setup:

331
00:20:07,602 --> 00:20:10,346
lockfile sync and hook install together.

332
00:20:10,666 --> 00:20:11,818
Pause and run it.

333
00:20:12,138 --> 00:20:14,932
The walk's next command
prints the hook file:

334
00:20:14,932 --> 00:20:18,410
the same hand-over chain as before,
with one difference.

335
00:20:18,730 --> 00:20:21,541
This machine's own venv path
is baked inside,

336
00:20:21,541 --> 00:20:23,103
so it is never committed.

337
00:20:23,350 --> 00:20:28,346
The config here has four hooks, not nine:
large files, merge markers,

338
00:20:28,346 --> 00:20:33,995
a policy hook refusing any .env file,
and one local hook that runs just check.

339
00:20:34,315 --> 00:20:36,598
So the hook runs exactly what you type.

340
00:20:36,918 --> 00:20:39,837
At this step, just check means format check,

341
00:20:39,837 --> 00:20:42,869
lint, tests with a 90 percent
coverage floor,

342
00:20:42,869 --> 00:20:44,150
and config linting.

343
00:20:44,650 --> 00:20:49,023
Run just check: everything green,
the floor reads 100 percent.

344
00:20:49,343 --> 00:20:50,468
Now watch it bite:

345
00:20:50,468 --> 00:20:53,781
ask for only the confidence tests,
keeping the floor.

346
00:20:54,101 --> 00:20:57,966
9 tests pass,
half the code is never exercised,

347
00:20:57,966 --> 00:21:01,503
and the run fails at 49.4 percent coverage.

348
00:21:01,823 --> 00:21:03,765
A floor is a gate, not a goal.

349
00:21:04,085 --> 00:21:05,386
Pause and run both.

350
00:21:05,634 --> 00:21:10,135
Config files are checked too:
a broken hook config breaks every commit.

351
00:21:10,455 --> 00:21:15,554
One wrong indent in the pre-commit config,
and yamllint names the exact line.

352
00:21:15,874 --> 00:21:18,366
Restored, the recipe runs green;

353
00:21:18,366 --> 00:21:22,807
it also validates pyproject.toml
and checks the lockfile.

354
00:21:23,050 --> 00:21:26,634
Now the gate refuses, twice;
pause and try the first.

355
00:21:26,954 --> 00:21:32,543
An unused import goes in;
the commit fails on F401, no commit made.

356
00:21:32,863 --> 00:21:34,851
Then a .env file is staged,

357
00:21:34,851 --> 00:21:38,165
and the refusal
is the config's own sentence:

358
00:21:38,165 --> 00:21:42,378
a .env file holds the settings
and secrets of one machine.

359
00:21:42,698 --> 00:21:44,106
It is never committed.

360
00:21:44,500 --> 00:21:48,914
Two gate styles, both fine:
this one points and you run just fmt;

361
00:21:48,914 --> 00:21:51,903
the cheese gate rewrites and makes you look.

362
00:21:52,223 --> 00:21:55,868
Steps 4 and 4b put
documentation in the gate:

363
00:21:55,868 --> 00:22:01,297
first 50 of 88 functions,
methods and classes typed and documented,

364
00:22:01,297 --> 00:22:06,644
one step later 88 of 88;
deleting one description blocks a commit.

365
00:22:06,964 --> 00:22:08,650
Two rows join the table.

366
00:22:13,783 --> 00:22:19,689
Step 5 brings the model:
the setup recipe now pulls the model stack,

367
00:22:19,689 --> 00:22:26,028
38 packages including torch 2.14.1
for the CPU, at the time of recording.

368
00:22:26,348 --> 00:22:31,234
Then just test:
92 passed, coverage 94 percent, all green.

369
00:22:31,554 --> 00:22:32,855
Pause and run both.

370
00:22:33,150 --> 00:22:34,558
What does the model do?

371
00:22:34,878 --> 00:22:39,017
A request packs one state
and several questions into one sequence.

372
00:22:39,337 --> 00:22:41,031
A mask enforces one rule:

373
00:22:41,031 --> 00:22:45,502
a question may see the state and itself,
never a sibling question.

374
00:22:45,822 --> 00:22:50,963
Questions must be answered independently;
a peek would leak one answer into another.

375
00:22:51,283 --> 00:22:55,102
That one forbidden cell
is the whole difference from a causal mask.

376
00:22:56,616 --> 00:22:59,216
The walk prints the mask the code builds,

377
00:22:59,216 --> 00:23:02,177
for two state tokens
and two questions of two;

378
00:23:02,177 --> 00:23:03,592
pause and print yours.

379
00:23:03,912 --> 00:23:08,840
Rows 5 and 6 say question 2 sees everything,
including question 1.

380
00:23:09,160 --> 00:23:10,672
The cause is one line:

381
00:23:10,672 --> 00:23:15,070
torch.ones_like marks every pair of tokens
as the same question.

382
00:23:15,316 --> 00:23:19,091
A checker script compares the built mask
against the one we meant,

383
00:23:19,091 --> 00:23:20,863
and prints WRONG on those rows.

384
00:23:21,183 --> 00:23:26,068
But nothing in just check runs it;
a printout you read by eye is not a test.

385
00:23:26,388 --> 00:23:27,821
Something to ponder:

386
00:23:27,821 --> 00:23:32,191
every line of branch_mask ran,
and coverage reads 94 percent.

387
00:23:32,511 --> 00:23:34,666
Can any coverage number catch this bug?

388
00:23:43,500 --> 00:23:49,175
No. Coverage counts lines that ran, never
whether anyone checked what they produced.

389
00:23:49,495 --> 00:23:51,649
Necessary, and never sufficient.

390
00:23:51,883 --> 00:23:54,352
Also green and blind: the type checker,

391
00:23:54,352 --> 00:23:57,643
which reads declared types
without running the code.

392
00:23:57,963 --> 00:24:01,846
Running ty finds 6 diagnostics,
yet just check is green:

393
00:24:01,846 --> 00:24:03,954
types are not in the gate yet,

394
00:24:03,954 --> 00:24:07,115
and a check outside
the gate protects nobody.

395
00:24:07,350 --> 00:24:11,705
Last, the smoke on the real model:
half a billion parameters,

396
00:24:11,705 --> 00:24:16,203
downloaded once from the Hugging Face Hub,
a public model host,

397
00:24:16,203 --> 00:24:20,129
answering 15 cheese questions on CPU;
pause and run it.

398
00:24:20,449 --> 00:24:21,942
6 of 15 right.

399
00:24:22,262 --> 00:24:25,568
Nothing is trained, these are guesses;
hold that number.

400
00:24:27,217 --> 00:24:29,990
Step 5a answers with tests that could tell.

401
00:24:30,310 --> 00:24:31,499
First, honesty:

402
00:24:31,499 --> 00:24:35,622
on this machine just test says 1 failed,
102 passed.

403
00:24:35,942 --> 00:24:39,356
The failing test demands bit
for bit equal outputs.

404
00:24:39,676 --> 00:24:45,188
On this Linux CPU, torch sums
in a different order than the author's Mac;

405
00:24:45,188 --> 00:24:48,209
there all 103 pass, as the walk records.

406
00:24:48,529 --> 00:24:51,642
Hermetic means only your code
can make it fail;

407
00:24:51,642 --> 00:24:55,086
bit exactness across machines
is a stronger promise;

408
00:24:55,086 --> 00:24:56,742
this machine measured it.

409
00:24:56,984 --> 00:24:58,093
Now the lesson.

410
00:24:58,413 --> 00:25:00,489
A deliberately wrong fix goes in,

411
00:25:00,489 --> 00:25:03,384
one that blocks only
the question just before.

412
00:25:03,704 --> 00:25:07,245
The six token picture says all ok;
it cannot tell.

413
00:25:07,565 --> 00:25:11,064
A four token picture can:
its last row reads WRONG.

414
00:25:11,300 --> 00:25:13,071
State the rule once instead.

415
00:25:13,391 --> 00:25:17,860
The property test lets hypothesis
draw any request shape:

416
00:25:17,860 --> 00:25:22,095
a state of 1 to 6 tokens,
up to 4 questions of 1 to 5.

417
00:25:22,415 --> 00:25:27,684
Those sizes allow 4,686 shapes,
too many for pictures.

418
00:25:28,004 --> 00:25:31,161
It asserts
that no allowed pair is ever a sibling pair.

419
00:25:31,481 --> 00:25:33,636
Something to ponder before we run it.

420
00:25:42,484 --> 00:25:44,691
You need a question two steps back,

421
00:25:44,691 --> 00:25:48,287
so one state token
and three questions of one token each.

422
00:25:48,607 --> 00:25:52,297
And hypothesis finds it on its own:
the one test fails,

423
00:25:52,297 --> 00:25:55,988
then shrinks the counter example
to exactly that shape.

424
00:25:56,308 --> 00:25:57,460
Pause and run it.

425
00:25:57,780 --> 00:26:02,174
A property test does not only say no;
it hands you the smallest witness.

426
00:26:02,417 --> 00:26:04,133
Proof the gate grew teeth:

427
00:26:04,133 --> 00:26:08,753
put step 5's wrong mask line back
and run just test; pause and try it.

428
00:26:09,073 --> 00:26:13,894
8 failed, 95 passed;
code that sailed through before is refused.

429
00:26:14,214 --> 00:26:17,030
A gate refuses only what its tests can see.

430
00:26:17,350 --> 00:26:21,809
The real fix is one line:
same question means same segment ID.

431
00:26:22,050 --> 00:26:27,554
Types join the gate too: the new recipe,
just types, passes, and check has six lines.

432
00:26:27,874 --> 00:26:29,837
And the smoke, with the right mask?

433
00:26:30,157 --> 00:26:32,397
4 of 15, lower than before.

434
00:26:32,717 --> 00:26:34,231
Pause and run the smoke.

435
00:26:34,551 --> 00:26:37,602
The 6 was luck;
an untrained score means nothing.

436
00:26:39,250 --> 00:26:42,663
Step 5b gives non-hermetic tests
an honest home.

437
00:26:42,983 --> 00:26:45,836
A new real model test is marked system,

438
00:26:45,836 --> 00:26:49,346
and the config deselects
that marker by default:

439
00:26:49,346 --> 00:26:52,711
every just test says 1 deselected,
on purpose.

440
00:26:52,950 --> 00:26:54,666
Asked for explicitly,

441
00:26:54,666 --> 00:26:58,751
just test -m system says 1 passed,
103 deselected:

442
00:26:58,751 --> 00:27:02,699
the real model ran,
and it had to pass, not skip.

443
00:27:03,019 --> 00:27:06,091
That is the honest version
of part 1's silent skip.

444
00:27:06,411 --> 00:27:08,118
Pause and run both forms.

445
00:27:08,367 --> 00:27:12,570
Smaller rows join the gate:
hadolint lints the Dockerfile, clean.

446
00:27:12,890 --> 00:27:16,561
Part 2 was recorded
on a plain machine without Docker,

447
00:27:16,561 --> 00:27:18,533
so the recipes fail politely:

448
00:27:18,533 --> 00:27:23,428
docker build is command not found,
and the image recipe explains itself.

449
00:27:23,748 --> 00:27:28,100
On your laptop, with Docker Desktop,
both images build for real.

450
00:27:28,420 --> 00:27:30,255
The walk's record shows the rest.

451
00:27:30,575 --> 00:27:36,143
Two images
from one lockfile, 1.15 and 1.84 gigabytes.

452
00:27:36,463 --> 00:27:40,545
And just check runs green inside a container
with no network:

453
00:27:40,545 --> 00:27:43,289
the hermetic claim, proved by experiment.

454
00:27:43,533 --> 00:27:47,906
The gate table is full:
six rows, system outside the turnstile.

455
00:27:48,226 --> 00:27:49,314
Now the limit.

456
00:27:49,634 --> 00:27:51,587
A hook runs on one machine,

457
00:27:51,587 --> 00:27:55,203
and git commit --no-verify walks
straight past it.

458
00:27:57,167 --> 00:28:01,952
So CI re-runs the same checks
on a server nobody can skip:

459
00:28:01,952 --> 00:28:05,252
part 5 of this same repo, in lecture 21.

460
00:28:05,572 --> 00:28:10,467
It is also why the hook, CI and the README
should spell the checks one way;

461
00:28:10,467 --> 00:28:13,274
the cheese repo does not quite manage that.

462
00:28:13,594 --> 00:28:17,263
One command for everyone
is the lesson for your milestone.

463
00:28:17,583 --> 00:28:21,495
Coverage floors in one line:
babykev chose 90;

464
00:28:21,495 --> 00:28:23,961
milestone 5 asks at least 70.

465
00:28:25,600 --> 00:28:27,264
Before the recap, clean up.

466
00:28:27,584 --> 00:28:29,422
The README's Docker cleanup:

467
00:28:29,422 --> 00:28:33,493
docker container ls shows nothing running,
so nothing to stop.

468
00:28:33,813 --> 00:28:35,660
Then docker system prune;

469
00:28:35,660 --> 00:28:40,683
type y: about 95 megabytes of build cache,
at the time of recording.

470
00:28:41,003 --> 00:28:44,757
A final docker image ls
shows the two cheese images.

471
00:28:45,077 --> 00:28:48,384
One extra undoes the hook:
pre-commit uninstall.

472
00:28:48,704 --> 00:28:52,896
On the babykev side, delete the venv;
and if you ran the smoke,

473
00:28:52,896 --> 00:28:56,555
the roughly 1 gigabyte model
in the Hugging Face cache.

474
00:28:56,875 --> 00:28:58,509
Nothing today was billable:

475
00:28:58,509 --> 00:29:01,717
the gate lives on your laptop,
and that is the point.

476
00:29:01,967 --> 00:29:03,972
5 questions to test yourself.

477
00:29:04,292 --> 00:29:07,073
One: what runs in a unit, an integration

478
00:29:07,073 --> 00:29:10,202
and a system test of this repo,
file by file?

479
00:29:10,522 --> 00:29:13,658
Two:
the system tests pass with no server at all.

480
00:29:13,978 --> 00:29:17,945
What does pytest actually print,
and are those four tests hermetic?

481
00:29:18,183 --> 00:29:21,449
Three: what does pre-commit install
actually create,

482
00:29:21,449 --> 00:29:23,836
and why must every clone run it again?

483
00:29:24,156 --> 00:29:27,250
Four: black says failed,
files were modified.

484
00:29:27,570 --> 00:29:30,108
Is your code broken,
and what do you do next?

485
00:29:30,428 --> 00:29:34,759
Five: babykev had 94 percent coverage
and a wrong mask.

486
00:29:35,079 --> 00:29:39,453
What kind of test finally saw the bug,
and why was coverage blind to it?

487
00:29:39,983 --> 00:29:44,676
The answers are in the exercises,
and every command is in the study guide.

488
00:29:44,996 --> 00:29:47,335
Next lecture brings cloud credentials,

489
00:29:47,335 --> 00:29:50,351
where the detect-private-key hook
earns its keep.

490
00:29:50,671 --> 00:29:55,030
And in lecture 21,
this very repo's checks run on GitHub,

491
00:29:55,030 --> 00:29:58,778
where nobody can walk past them
